      },
      {
        "text": "This concern is causing a rift in negotiating concrete, credible reforms to bolster the workforce and economy.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Fear-based narratives around immigration policy are often used strategically to influence public opinion.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "There exists a consensus among policymakers that temporary work permits threaten American job opportunities.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes the guest’s political positioning on immigration is courageous given historical precedents.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "The guest has firsthand experience with crafting immigration-related policies due to their role as a politician.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      },
      {
        "text": "Audiences trust the speaker’s framing of immigration debates as being driven by self-interest rather than genuine concern for national welfare.",
        "confidence": 0.83
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 72487,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72487.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 73,
    "turn_text": "Well, one time my district is nearly half of the southern border. The other part of it, there is not one state in the United States that hasn't become a border state in some form or fashion. And I think we can solve both of these issues. What we see is politically lucrative to blame the other party for everything that's wrong. And people run that play over and over. On one end you have folks that say we can't give in to border security because folks won't have pathway to citizenship. On the other end you say folks that go, we can't give in to pathway to citizenship because we'll never get border security. Well, my bill doesn't attach either of those. Hey, you can still go in your corner and fight over those things. What this bill focuses on is people that want to come over legally to work. It doesn't add, you know, social services or the ability to vote. It removes some of the politics in it and it focuses on work visas. We have shortages in every single industry. I think it's important that we have more of these conversations, real, tangible conversations to move this country forward.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_19",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
